      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dyke earned its name in the 19th century after a family of enslaved people with West African roots who once lived here.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyke%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Early settlers in Dyke included the Parrott, Gibson, Austin, Shifflet, Ogg, and White families.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyke%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Binghams United Methodist Church, once Austin's Meeting House, was founded in 1796 on the north side of the Lynch River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
